<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  hexapod.gazebo.xacro : simulation-only sensors and ground truth.
  ==========================================================================
  WHY GROUND TRUTH ODOMETRY IS NOT OPTIONAL HERE

  Right now there is no way to tell whether the robot is WALKING or merely
  CYCLING ITS LEGS. RViz cannot answer the question: its fixed frame is
  base_link, and base_link is the robot, so by construction the robot never
  appears to move. Everything else moves around it. A robot skidding in
  place and a robot striding forward look identical.

  That is not a display setting to fix; it is a missing frame. Nothing in
  the stack currently knows where the body is in the world. robot_state
  publisher only knows joint angles, so it can only produce transforms from
  base_link downwards.

  The p3d plugin ("position 3D") reads the body's true pose directly out of
  the physics engine and publishes it as nav_msgs/Odometry. On real hardware
  this would have to come from an IMU plus leg odometry, or a motion capture
  rig. In simulation we can simply ask.

  Ground truth is exactly what you want for VERIFICATION, as opposed to for
  control. It lets you answer the only question that matters about a gait:

      does the achieved body velocity match the commanded body velocity?

  and it gives you a plot for the report that is far more convincing than a
  video. Do not feed it back into a controller: that would be cheating, and
  it would hide precisely the drift a real robot suffers.
==========================================================================  -->
<robot xmlns:xacro="http://www.ros.org/wiki/xacro">

  <gazebo>
    <plugin name="ground_truth_odom" filename="libgazebo_ros_p3d.so">
      <ros>
        <namespace>/</namespace>
        <remapping>odom:=odom</remapping>
      </ros>

      <!-- Which link to report. base_link is the robot's root, so its pose
           is the body pose. -->
      <body_name>base_link</body_name>

      <!-- Report relative to the world origin, i.e. absolute position. -->
      <frame_name>world</frame_name>

      <update_rate>50.0</update_rate>

      <!-- Zero noise: this is a measurement instrument, not a simulated
           sensor. If you later want to test robustness to noisy state
           estimation, add noise HERE rather than degrading the controller,
           so the two effects stay separable. -->
      <gaussian_noise>0.0</gaussian_noise>
    </plugin>
  </gazebo>

</robot>
